stream
BT
/F1 20.00 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 742.00 Tm (Chapter 3 Data Replication) Tj
/F1 10.00 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 728.00 Tm (Replication keeps the same data on several nodes to improve availability, lower read latency and spread read traffic.) Tj
/F1 10.00 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 714.00 Tm (Every write has to travel to more than one machine, so consistency and write amplification become the price.) Tj
/F1 14.00 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 694.40 Tm (3.1 Leader-Follower Replication) Tj
/F1 10.00 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 680.40 Tm (All writes go through the leader, which streams its change log to followers as binlog or write-ahead log entries.) Tj
/F1 10.00 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 666.40 Tm (Asynchronous replication keeps write latency low but can lose the most recent writes when the leader dies.) Tj
/F1 10.00 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 652.40 Tm (Synchronous replication avoids data loss, yet one slow follower is enough to stall every write on the leader.) Tj
/F1 10.00 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 638.40 Tm (Semi-synchronous replication is the common compromise: the leader waits for a single follower acknowledgement.) Tj
/F1 10.00 Tf 1 0 0 1 72 624.40 Tm (Promoting a follower after a crash needs a consensus service to avoid two nodes believing they are the leader.) Tj
ET
endstream
endobj
